## 6. Restrictions and Special Situations

### 6.1 Restricted Leave Periods

The company may restrict annual leave during the following critical periods:
- **Month-end and Quarter-end:** Last 5 days of each month and last week of each quarter (business-critical for finance/sales teams)
- **Product Release/Launch:** 1 week before and 2 weeks after major product releases
- **Customer Events:** During planned customer engagement or onboarding activities
- **Fiscal Year-End:** Last 2 weeks of December (preparation for audit, budgeting)

**Notification:**
- Restricted periods are communicated at least 2 weeks in advance
- Employees should plan leave around these periods
- Exceptions may be granted with VP approval for compelling reasons (pre-booked vacation, family emergency)

### 6.2 Leave During Probation Period

**Probationary Employees:**
- Entitled to the same leave as regular employees
- Annual, casual, and sick leave are available during probation
- For extended leave (maternity, adoption, unpaid >1 week), approval from the manager and VP is required
- Taking excessive leave during probation may negatively affect the probation review

### 6.3 Leave During Performance Improvement Plan (PIP)

- Employees on a PIP may be required to work during the critical improvement period
- Leave may be restricted or deferred; exceptions may be made for medical reasons
- Any leave during PIP should be pre-approved by the manager and HR

### 6.4 Combining Leave Types

**Not Allowed:**
- Using sick leave immediately before/after annual leave to extend vacation
- Combining multiple leave types to circumvent restrictions
- If a pattern emerges, the manager will discuss and HR may require medical certification

**Allowed:**
- Combining half-day annual leave with half-day casual leave (with manager approval)
- Taking sick leave for actual illness regardless of adjacent leave (with medical certification if pattern is suspected)

